WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.070
SPEAKER 1: Building
interoperability

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.320
across systems in health
care poses unique challenges,

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.530
especially if they are siloed.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.260
This is where the health
care API helps accelerate

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.740
the uploading, sending,
analyzing, storing,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.110
and interfacing with
other health care

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.540
data via cloud-based
applications.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.520
And so in this episode,
we will talk about

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.140
what is a health care API?

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.215
And what are some best
practices around using FHIR?

00:00:25.215 --> 00:00:27.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.825 --> 00:00:33.990
API stands for Application
Programming Interface

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.960
and is a kind of library
of functions that passes

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.670
information over the web.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:43.170
It defines the inputs and
outputs available over HTTP.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.320
The health care API specifically
bundles multiple tools

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.800
and processes that help
application developers deliver

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.770
compliance in a heavily
regulated environment,

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.470
as well as store sensitive
patient information

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.290
in a more convenient
and standard manner.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.150
And even though
you enable it like

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.720
you do with any other API
in the Google Cloud console,

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.720
the health care API contains
specialized data-storing

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.660
modalities in order to support
DICOM, HL7v2, and FHIR.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.290
DICOM means Digital Imaging
and Communications in Medicine,

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.560
and we will talk more about
that in another episode.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.070
Next is HL7v2, which is an
event messaging service that

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.010
was created in 1989 for
health care entities

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.740
to communicate with each other--
such as medical providers,

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.510
labs, insurers, et cetera.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.640
Now, there are two common
challenges, however.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.160
The first is that it's
semi-structured data,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.810
and that is difficult to parse.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.660
And the second is that it
uses a protocol that predates

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.030
the internet's HTTP standard.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.710
To overcome these,
the health care

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.350
API can store the
messages in its raw format

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.690
or parse the messages
for developers

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.830
to more easily work with it.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:53.640
And one can overcome the
protocols web constraints

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.310
by also using a free
adapter in tandem

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.410
with the health care API.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.710
Doing this also then expands
your ability to leverage

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.570
additional Cloud
infrastructure tools,

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:06.450
which can help process
massive amounts of data

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.449
to find insights or
apply machine learning.

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:12.630
Next, moving on to FHIR, which
stands for Fast Health Care

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.590
Interoperability Resources.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.370
This is an API and data
standard that came about

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.320
as the industry has been
working on improving

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.630
the exchange of
electronic health records

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.650
by leveraging modern web-based
technologies like OAuth, JSON,

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:27.120
and HTML.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.920
The health care
API has the benefit

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.280
of mapping data as a graph
or a web of resources

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.460
that are interconnected, and
you can perform human queries

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:36.850
over them.

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:38.940
Let's take a quick look.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.720
SPEAKER 2: On top of a robust
set of API functionalities,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.390
the health care team has
built a user interface

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:48.930
to interact with Google
Cloud's health care API.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:53.040
Here I am looking at the Dataset
view, a way for organizations

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.870
to logically partition
their resources.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.320
If I click in, I
have the ability

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.980
to create data stores
right within the interface.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:08.040
Data stores can include
Consent, DICOM, HL7, and FHIR.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.030
When creating a FHIR store,
I can attach a Cloud Pub/Sub

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.940
topic for triggering
downstream workflows.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.050
This will essentially fire off
when there are any changes made

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.970
to the back-end database.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.510
I can also attach a label
for use cases like billing

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:29.160
and choose which version of
FHIR I would like to utilize.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.830
Back in the Dataset
view, I have the ability

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:37.530
to import, export to both Google
Cloud Storage and BigQuery,

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:43.440
de-identify my data, delete,
or open up into a FHIR viewer.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.320
This FHIR viewer is
a interface to be

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.350
able to see the data and
interact with the data

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:53.250
without directly invoking
the back-end API.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.830
We can see, our
resources are listed.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:59.470
And I'll be searching for
the Encounter resource.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:01.770
This is listing all
of the encounters

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:04.530
I have within my FHIR store.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:09.270
If I click in, we can see
there's an Overview, Elements,

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.550
and JSON tab.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.100
The Elements is a
logical representation

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.380
of the raw JSON object.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.209
We still have the
ability to view it

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:22.500
as a JSON object if your
developers would prefer.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.770
Within Elements, each
of these is a dropdown.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.020
So for example, when
looking at reasonCode,

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:33.810
we can see there's a snomed code
associated with this encounter.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.330
We also have the
ability to check things

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:38.520
like referential
integrity, which

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.760
is the ability to refer
to another resource

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.560
in the FHIR store.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:48.510
This is currently telling me
this practitioner was the owner

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:52.590
or who saw the person
within this encounter.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:56.160
We can click the practitioner
and actually go view their data

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.420
in the dataset.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:02.460
So not only do we a robust
set of implementations

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:07.380
within the FHIR API, we also
have developer-friendly tools

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.110
to better leverage the product.

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:13.230
FHIR's been getting a lot
of buzz in the market today

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:16.080
due to its promise in
interoperability and US

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.880
legislation changes.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:21.480
One of the main benefits that
we see in organizations that

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.700
move towards a
FHIR-based data model

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.360
is the ability to unlock
real-time use cases.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.690
Google's FHIR server is
built on the same technology

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.390
that Cloud Spanner is
built on and run on.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:37.080
Thus, this allows for high
transactions and throughput,

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.380
both necessary things for
AI analytics use cases.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:44.190
One of the biggest
considerations an organization

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:47.160
should make when adopting
a FHIR-based standard

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.200
is how well it scales.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.470
Ideally, a FHIR store
should be able to hold

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:56.190
all an organization's
data without a large loss

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:57.600
in performance.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.330
That's one of the best
things about managed products

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.680
and Google Cloud's FHIR API.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.250
It will automatically
scale to meet your workload

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.530
and your data storage needs.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.510
That way, an
organization can use it

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.970
for any number of use
cases without having

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.760
a need for data replication
or bringing latency

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.560
into their applications.

